From 251c40b866ec21f13ba4841204938203a50664fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fritchii Intel fixes <intel-fixes@users.noreply.invalid>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:11:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] spirv: native float32 storage atomic add on Intel
 (VK_EXT_shader_atomic_float)

Every storage f32 atomic add was a compare-exchange loop. OoT's 1e1964184a291f00 (96 workgroups
summing into a few words) spent 1.8-2.0 ms of every frame in it on an Arc B580, which has
hardware float atomic add.

On Intel with shaderBufferFloat32AtomicAdd the add is one OpAtomicFAddEXT on an aliased F32 view
of the buffer (emitted as OpAtomicFMaxEXT, which has the same words, and rewritten in
RewriteOpcodes, since Sirit has no FAdd). EDEN_NX2_NO_FLOAT_ATOMICS=1 keeps the loop. Limited to
Intel because it was only measured there; likely fine on NVIDIA/AMD too.

Verified: spirv-val; offline outputs match the loop (8 of 8.4 M values differ in the last bit
from addition order, which also varies run to run with the loop); in game the shader left the
GPU's top list, image unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
